  "notes_for_editor": "Keepers: the roster was taken from the archive's place record by roster.py (94 postings, 1905-1965; 'at least' spans kept as approximate; 23 Coast Guard postings with no position stated; person slugs from the archive). Later Coast Guard names are published by the archive; the story names none after 1950. First-lit date 15 Feb 1906 and the 1905 keeper arrival rest on LighthouseFriends; a newspaper check of February 1906 was not made. Sinking time: NOAA says within 10 minutes, LighthouseFriends eight; the story gives both. The record's status 'active' conflicts with the archive's 'Active Aid to Navigation? No' and 'Existing: No'; left for an editor. The record's focal height 14.9 m does not match the archive's 78.25 ft (about 23.8 m) for the original light above mean high water; probably the present light; not changed. The 1919 Bulletin says 'On February 1' without the year in that sentence; the year is taken from the 1919 volume. The 1919 Bulletin's index (PDF p. 16) lists the rescuer as 'Hicks, W.B.'; the rescue text reads 'W. H. Hicks' and the story follows the text and the archive roster. Atlas Obscura's 1904 build date is consistent with the 1904 contract and was not cited. The archive's place record gives keepers' quarters 'Concrete' (1906) while roster.py reads keepers_quarters false; the story does not rely on either. Wikipedia used for leads only.",
